Traditional OOP (Java-style):

    Animal (abstract class)
       |
       +-- Mammal (abstract class)
              |
              +-- Dog
              +-- Cat

Problem: Adding "CanSwim" behavior requires
modifying the hierarchy or using multiple inheritance.

---

Go's Approach:

    Dog struct {
        name string
    }
    func (d Dog) Speak() { ... }
    func (d Dog) Swim() { ... }

    Cat struct {
        name string
    }
    func (c Cat) Speak() { ... }

    // Interfaces defined by consumers, not producers
    type Speaker interface { Speak() }
    type Swimmer interface { Swim() }

Dog satisfies both Speaker and Swimmer.
Cat satisfies only Speaker.
No inheritance needed.

